  "strategy": "Rationale: We'll use a genetic algorithm approach with a focus on optimization, catering to both the provided parent sequence and a base case. The algorithm aims to minimize the evaluation function's output by evolving a population of step function coefficients, adapting crossover and mutation strategies dynamically. We'll incorporate:\n1. Tournament selection for efficient candidate generation.\n2. Two-point crossover to maintain structural integrity.\n3. Smart mutation using subgradient information from convolution.\n4. An extremal trapezoid generator to provide structured solutions when improvements plateau.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe GA searches for sequences of coefficients that minimize:\n    f(sequence) = 2 * n * \\|self-convolve(sequence)\\|_\u221e / (\\|sequence\\|^2)\nwith \\|sequence\\| = sum(sequence). The initial population is derived from log-normal and uniform distributions, then normalized to sqrt(2 * n) to control for scale. Fitness uses numpy's full convolution. Decision variables are the coefficient values, and the constraints enforce nonnegative values and the normalization.\n\nImplementation procedure:\nWe begin by establishing parameters: population size 50, generations up to 1800, tournament size 3, mutation probability 0.5. Handle parent sequences by initial normalizing. Evaluate fitness via full convolution max. Use tournament selection to guide mutations and crossovers. After mutation, cap values to ensure they remain within [0, 1000]. When evaluating fitness, use `evaluate_sequence` to compute the exact objective.\n\nFor the initial population, if provided:\n    - Normalize the parent's elements to uniform or log-normal distributions.\nIf not:\n    - Sample sequences from [0.001, 1000.001] using a hybrid approach.\n\nSelection and mutation:\n- Tournament selection: find worst fitness candidate for mutation targeting.\n- Mutation using subgradient (3-point targeted): \n    a. Neutral mutation maintains sum, introduces zeroed or negated elements where coefficients support the peak.\n    b. Smart mutation adjusts coefficients based on self-correlation peak gradients, favors direction.\n\nCrossover:\n- Two-point crossover: For candidate pairs, cut randomly at two points and swap segments.\n\nThe algorithm runs for up to 1800 seconds, checks time per generation, and dynamically adjusts search strategy. If convergence stalls (no improvement for 200 generations), it resamples the population using an extremal trapezoid generator (0s, 1s, 0s...). Finally, returns the best candidate evaluated by `evaluate_sequence`.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n- Start by normalizing provided parent or random template.\n- Use tournament selection (every 20 generations) to pick candidates.\n- Vary mutation probability between generations as a fallback if needed.\n\nComputation budget:\nEach fitness evaluation performs an exact convolution using `evaluate_sequence`. With limited computing resources, continuous monitoring is applied to ensure timely completion.\n\nValidation:\n- Ensure sequence length is 256 (or parent) and all elements nonnegative.\n- Return best sequence via `evaluate_sequence`.\n\nThe final code is:",
